    {
      "beat_id": "B01",
      "estimated_duration_s": 12,
      "narration_text": "This is Liam, in for Bear. Clifford circuits can create vast superpositions and entanglement yet still admit efficient classical tracking. Add non-Clifford T gates, and simulation typically becomes harder as their number grows — not because one T gate instantly destroys every simulator.",
      "shot": {
        "type": "CARD",
        "source": "remotion",
        "motion": "type",
        "remotion": {
          "pattern": "ClaudeComposerAsk",
          "props": {
            "command": "Explain the classical-simulation boundary between Clifford and T gates.",
            "topic": "QUANTUM MECHANICS — SIMULATION",
            "segment": "Clifford Plus T",
            "greeting": "Kia ora, Liam",
            "runningText": "tracking stabilizers and non-Clifford resources…",
            "output": [
              "Clifford-only circuits: compact stabilizer tracking.",
              "Many T gates: growing non-stabilizer simulation cost."
            ],
            "folderLabel": "@NikBearBrown",
            "modelLabel": "Claude",
            "effortLabel": "High"
          },
          "rendered": {
            "out": "media/B01.mp4",
            "at": ""
          }
        }
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B01.mp3",
      "build": {
        "status": "VIDEO",
        "src": "media/B01.mp4",
        "filled_by": "media",
        "at": "2026-07-18T23:45:29"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B02",
      "estimated_duration_s": 11,
      "narration_text": "The Clifford gate set includes Hadamard, phase S, and controlled-NOT. These gates map Pauli operators to Pauli operators under conjugation.",
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "own",
        "motion": "branch"
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B02.mp3",
      "build": {
        "status": "MANIM",
        "src": "manim/B02.mp4",
        "filled_by": "manim",
        "at": "2026-07-18T23:45:29"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B03",
      "estimated_duration_s": 11,
      "narration_text": "A stabilizer state can therefore be represented by a compact list of Pauli constraints. Clifford gates update that list with polynomial classical work instead of storing all two-to-the-n amplitudes.",
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "own",
        "motion": "transform"
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B03.mp3",
      "build": {
        "status": "MANIM",
        "src": "manim/B03.mp4",
        "filled_by": "manim",
        "at": "2026-07-18T23:45:29"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B04",
      "estimated_duration_s": 11,
      "narration_text": "That is the Gottesman-Knill lesson: a broad class of Clifford state preparations, Clifford evolution, and Pauli measurements is efficiently classically simulable under its stated conditions.",
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "own",
        "motion": "threshold"
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B04.mp3",
      "build": {
        "status": "MANIM",
        "src": "manim/B04.mp4",
        "filled_by": "manim",
        "at": "2026-07-18T23:45:29"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B05",
      "estimated_duration_s": 10,
      "narration_text": "Superposition and entanglement are therefore not sufficient by themselves for generic quantum computational advantage. Clifford circuits can contain both.",
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "own",
        "motion": "compare"
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B05.mp3",
      "build": {
        "status": "MANIM",
        "src": "manim/B05.mp4",
        "filled_by": "manim",
        "at": "2026-07-18T23:45:29"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B06",
      "estimated_duration_s": 11,
      "narration_text": "The T gate is a pi-over-four phase rotation. It is non-Clifford: it does not preserve the Pauli stabilizer description in the same closed way.",
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "own",
        "motion": "morph"
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B06.mp3",
      "build": {
        "status": "MANIM",
        "src": "manim/B06.mp4",
        "filled_by": "manim",
        "at": "2026-07-18T23:45:29"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B07",
      "estimated_duration_s": 12,
      "narration_text": "One T gate is not catastrophic. Classical methods can expand a circuit into a small combination of stabilizer pieces. But the required representation can grow rapidly with the number and arrangement of non-Clifford gates.",
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "own",
        "motion": "accumulate"
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B07.mp3",
      "build": {
        "status": "MANIM",
        "src": "manim/B07.mp4",
        "filled_by": "manim",
        "at": "2026-07-18T23:45:29"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B08",
      "estimated_duration_s": 11,
      "narration_text": "That is why T count, T depth, and related measures of non-stabilizer resource matter in simulation and fault-tolerant compilation. The exact cost depends on the circuit and algorithm used.",
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "own",
        "motion": "compare"
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B08.mp3",
      "build": {
        "status": "MANIM",
        "src": "manim/B08.mp4",
        "filled_by": "manim",
        "at": "2026-07-18T23:45:29"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B09",
      "estimated_duration_s": 12,
      "narration_text": "Clifford plus T is also a universal gate set for approximating arbitrary quantum circuits. But universality does not prove that every such circuit is hard, and no single hardware-overhead number applies to every T gate implementation.",
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "own",
        "motion": "collapse"
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B09.mp3",
      "build": {
        "status": "MANIM",
        "src": "manim/B09.mp4",
        "filled_by": "manim",
        "at": "2026-07-18T23:45:29"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B10",
      "estimated_duration_s": 13,
      "narration_text": "Your turn. A fifty-qubit Clifford circuit gains one T gate. Must every classical simulator immediately run out of memory? Pause before the answer: no. One T is usually manageable; difficulty generally grows with accumulated non-Clifford resource and circuit structure.",
      "shot": {
        "type": "CARD",
        "source": "remotion",
        "motion": "type",
        "remotion": {
          "pattern": "ClaudeComposerAsk",
          "props": {
            "command": "Your turn: does one T gate guarantee simulation failure?",
            "topic": "QUANTUM MECHANICS — CHECK",
            "segment": "Resource, Not Switch",
            "greeting": "Over to you, Liam",
            "runningText": "waiting for the qualified claim…",
            "output": [
              "One T gate: generally still manageable.",
              "Hardness pressure grows with non-Clifford count, depth, and structure."
            ],
            "folderLabel": "@NikBearBrown",
            "modelLabel": "Claude",
            "effortLabel": "High"
          },
          "rendered": {
            "out": "media/B10.mp4",
            "at": ""
          }
        }
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B10.mp3",
      "build": {
        "status": "VIDEO",
        "src": "media/B10.mp4",
        "filled_by": "media",
        "at": "2026-07-18T23:45:29"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B11",
      "estimated_duration_s": 8,
      "narration_text": "The gate that breaks the compact stabilizer story is T — but simulation cost is a growing resource curve, not an instant on-off switch.",
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "own",
        "motion": "fade"
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B11.mp3",
